[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 1:36] Mm-hm. How would you prioritize these levers and validate the expected profit impact before recommending them?

[candidate · 1:43] I'd prioritize on impact times speed times reversibility.

First, menu pricing. Biggest and fastest. A 5% increase is roughly 5 points of margin at constant volume, which alone would take us from 6% to around 11%. Validation: run a pilot in 20 of the 200 stores across different markets, hold four to six weeks, and measure transaction count and ticket against matched control stores. That gives me a real elasticity read rather than an assumption.

Second, procurement and hedging on beans. Sized at about 4 points of revenue exposure. Validation: get supplier quotes on 12-month fixed contracts and compare against forward commodity curves.

Third, waste and portioning. Validation: time-and-motion study plus inventory reconciliation in a few stores to see actual versus theoretical usage.

Fourth, overhead and mix — smaller, slower, and I'd want the item-level margin data before touching mix.

I'd gate everything on the pilot. Pricing is the one lever I'd want hard evidence on before rolling to 200 stores.
